= Upgrading to Carve 0.2

== Profiles

`carve.profile` takes one of the four profile names: `full`, `article`, `comment` or `minimal`. Custom profiles are gone: a `Profile` object, a custom maximum length and allow lists have no 0.2 counterpart, and a config that passes one fails the build.

== Link policy and base host

In 0.1 a custom profile could carry a link policy, and the build passed the host of `hostname` to it as the base host. In 0.2 the link policy is the `carve.linkPolicy` config key, which takes the options of the `linkPolicy()` extension:

```ts
export default {
  title: 'Docs',
  hostname: 'https://example.com',
  carve: { linkPolicy: { allowExternal: false } },
}
```

The base host is still the host of `hostname`, without its port. With `allowExternal: false`, a link to `https://example.com/...` stays and a link to any other host is written as its text. Set `baseHost` in `carve.linkPolicy` to compare with another host.

The four named profiles never limited link hosts, so a site that only sets `carve.profile` renders its links as before.
